VATIONS & BENEFICIARIES',\n    45→      items: [\n    46→        'Who benefits financially? (defense contractors, corporations, governments)',\n    47→        'Market movements before and after related events',\n    48→        'Economic interests at stake',\n    49→        'Follow the money: funding, contracts, investments',\n    50→      ],\n    51→    },\n    52→    {\n    53→      name: 'ACTOR INTERESTS & STATED vs HIDDEN AGENDAS',\n    54→      items: [\n    55→        'Official positions vs revealed actions',\n    56→        'Stakeholder analysis (governments, corporations, NGOs)',\n    57→        'Declared objectives vs strategic interests',\n    58→      ],\n    59→    },\n    60→    {\n    61→      name: 'FACT-CHECKING & MISINFORMATION',\n    62→      items: [\n    63→        'Debunked claims about this topic',\n    64→        'Known propaganda narratives',\n    65→        'Corrections and retractions by media',\n    66→        'Primary source verification',\n    67→      ],\n    68→    },\n    69→    {\n    70→      name: 'ALTERNATIVE PERSPECTIVES',\n    71→      items: [\n    72→        'Non-mainstream analysis and commentary',\n    73→        'Local/regional reporting vs international coverage',\n    74→        'Academic and expert analysis',\n    75→        'Dissenting viewpoints',\n    76→      ],\n    77→    },\n    78→    {\n    79→      name: 'HISTORICAL PARALLELS',\n    80→      items: [\n    81→        'Similar events in history',\n    82→        'Pattern recognition across comparable situations',\n    83→        'Lessons from past events',\n    84→      ],\n    85→    },\n    86→  ],\n    87→\n    88→  searchDepthGuidance: {\n    89→    quick:\n    90→      'Focus on 4-5 most critical angles (chronology, media, financial, fact-check)',\n    91→    standard: 'Cover 6-7 angles with balanced depth',\n    92→    deep: 'Comprehensive coverage of all angles with follow-up queries',\n    93→  },\n    94→\n    95→  // ---- Extraction Phase Configuration ----\n    96→  extractionIntro:\n    97→    'You are an investigative analyst extracting findings to understand a major world event.',\n    98→\n    99→  findingTypes: [\n   100→    {\n   101→      name: 'event_chain',\n   102→      displayName: 'Event Chain',\n   103→      description:\n   104→        'Chronological events leading to the main event. Include: date, description, causal link to main event.',\n   105→      extractedDataSchema:\n   106→        '{\"event_date\": \"YYYY-MM-DD\", \"event_description\": \"\", \"causal_link\": \"\", \"established_fact\": true}',\n   107→      analysisFallback:\n   108→        'This event in the causal chain helps explain how the situation developed over time.',\n   109→    },\n   110→    {\n   111→      name: 'media_narrative',\n   112→      displayName: 'Media Narrative',\n   113→      description:\n   114→        'How specific outlets/media types covered this or predecessor events. Note any significant omissions or spin.',\n   115→      extractedDataSchema:\n   116→        '{\"outlet\": \"\", \"narrative_frame\": \"\", \"historical_accuracy\": \"high/medium/low\", \"noted_bias\": \"\", \"omissions\": \"\"}',\n   117→      analysisFallback:\n   118→        'This media framing reveals how different outlets are shaping public perception of the event.',\n   119→    },\n   120→    {\n   121→      name: 'financial_motivation',\n   122→      displayName: 'Financial Motivation',\n   123→      description:\n   124→        'Money flows, beneficiaries, economic interests. Be specific about HOW they benefit.',\n   125→      extractedDataSchema:\n   126→        '{\"actor\": \"\", \"benefit_type\": \"\", \"amount\": \"\", \"mechanism\": \"\", \"timing\": \"\"}',\n   127→      analysisFallback:\n   128→        \"This financial interest helps explain the underlying motivations behind key actors' positions.\",\n   129→    },\n   130→    {\n   131→      name: 'misinformation_pattern',\n   132→      displayName: 'Misinformation Pattern',\n   133→      description:\n   134→        'Detected false claims, propaganda techniques, debunked narratives.',\n   135→      extractedDataSchema:\n   136→        '{\"claim\": \"\", \"debunking_evidence\": \"\", \"propagators\": [], \"technique\": \"\", \"spread_level\": \"high/medium/low\"}',\n   137→      analysisFallback:\n   138→        'This misinformation pattern demonstrates active narrative manipulation that affects public understanding.',\n   139→    },\n   140→    {\n   141→      name: 'source_credibility',\n   142→      displayName: 'Source Credibility',\n   143→      description:\n   144→        'Assessment of source reliability based on historical coverage.',\n   145→      extractedDataSchema:\n   146→        '{\"source_name\": \"\", \"track_record\": \"good/mixed/poor\", \"notable_errors\": [], \"notable_successes\": [], \"ownership_bias\": \"\"}',\n   147→      analysisFallback:\n   148→        'This credibility assessment helps calibrate how much weight to give different sources.',\n   149→    },\n   150→    {\n   151→      name: 'actor_interest',\n   152→      displayName: 'Actor Interest',\n   153→      description: 'Stakeholders and their stated vs hidden interests.',\n   154→      extractedDataSchema:\n   155→        '{\"actor\": \"\", \"stated_position\": \"\", \"likely_interest\": \"\", \"evidence_for_hidden_interest\": \"\"}',\n   156→      analysisFallback:\n   157→        \"Understanding this actor's interests reveals the gap between stated positions and likely motivations.\",\n   158→    },\n   159→    {\n   160→      name: 'counter_narrative',\n   161→      displayName: 'Counter Narrative',\n   162→      description: 'Alternative explanations and dissenting views.',\n   163→      extractedDataSchema:\n   164→        '{\"alternative_view\": \"\", \"proponents\": [], \"evidence\": \"\", \"credibility_assessment\": \"\"}',\n   165→      analysisFallback:\n   166→        'This alternative perspective challenges mainstream assumptions and deserves consideration.',\n   167→    },\n   168→    {\n   169→      name: 'historical_parallel',\n   170→      displayName: 'Historical Parallel',\n   171→      description: 'Similar events in history that provide context.',\n   172→      extractedDataSchema:\n   173→        '{\"parallel_event\": \"\", \"date\": \"\", \"similarities\": [], \"differences\": [], \"lesson\": \"\"}',\n   174→      analysisFallback:\n   175→        'This historical comparison provides context for understanding likely outcomes and patterns.',\n   176→    },\n   177→  ],\n   178→\n   179→  extractionGuidelines: `CRITICAL: The \"analysis\" field must provide substantive reasoning, not just restate the finding.\n   180→Bad example: \"This is an important media narrative.\"\n   181→Good example: \"This narrative framing emerged specifically after the policy announcement, suggesting coordinated messaging. The choice to emphasize economic impacts while omitting human costs reflects the outlet's historical pattern of pro-business coverage. This contrasts sharply with local media reporting, which prioritized community impacts.\"\n   182→\n   183→IMPORTANT GUIDELINES:\n   184→- Be skeptical - flag claims that lack corroboration\n   185→- Distinguish between established facts and analysis/opinion\n   186→- Note conflicting narratives rather than choosing one\n   187→- Prioritize primary sources over secondary reporting\n   188→- Flag propaganda techniques (emotional manipulation, false equivalence, strawmen, etc.)`,\n   189→\n   190→  analysisInstruction: `YOUR EXPERT ANALYTICAL COMMENTARY (REQUIRED - 2-4 sentences) explaining:\n   191→  * WHY this finding matters for understanding the event/topic\n   192→  * What CONTEXT or BACKGROUND helps interpret this\n   193→  * How this RELATES to other findings or the broader narrative\n   194→  * What this REVEALS about hidden motivations, biases, or dynamics`,\n   195→\n   196→  // ---- Ordering & Grouping ----\n   197→  priorityFindingTypes: [\n   198→    'event_chain',\n   199→    'financial_motivation',\n   200→    'misinformation_pattern',\n   201→    'actor_interest',\n   202→    'media_narrative',\n   203→    'source_credibility',\n   204→  ],\n   205→  groupingOrder: [\n   206→    'event_chain',\n   207→    'financial_motivation',\n   208→    'misinformation_pattern',\n   209→    'actor_interest',\n   210→    'media_narrative',\n   211→    'source_credibility',\n   212→    'counter_narrative',\n   213→    'historical_parallel',\n   214→  ],\n   215→\n   216→  // ---- Perspectives ----\n   217→  // 6 expert perspectives for understanding complex events\n   218→  perspectives: [\n   219→    'media_analyst',\n   220→    'forensic_financial',\n   221→    'geopolitical_strategic',\n   222→    'fact_checker',\n   223→    'historian',\n   224→    'intelligence_analyst',\n   225→  ],\n   226→\n   227→  // ---- Verification ----\n   228→  // Understanding requires thorough verification on all dimensions\n   229→  verificationConfig: {\n   230→    crossReference: 'thorough',\n   231→    biasDetection: 'thorough',\n   232→    expertSanityCheck: 'thorough',\n   233→    sourceQuality: 'thorough',\n   234→  },\n   235→\n   236→  // ---- Resource Limits ----\n   237→  defaultMaxSearches: 10,\n   238→};\n   239→\n   240→export default understandingConfig;\n   241→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d184f0d2-244e-457f-8ea5-4627f2d647f1","uuid":"211446a3-b0af-40e9-8446-494eb0818df9","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\mkdol\\dolla\\res\\src\\templates\\research\\understanding.ts","content":"/**\n * Understanding Template Configuration\n *\n * Migrated from: a